    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The corners are a chaotic tapestry of fractured realities, populated primarily by an aging, overwhelmingly Black population who have carved out lifetimes of resilience and facing down cops, while a pervasive, wary presence of Latino families moves the streets like ghosts, their children often the latest targets of desperate drug runoff that snags at them from dark alleyways.\n\nHowever, beneath the heavy weight of decades of systemic neglect, you can't help but see the occasional glitch in the fabric—a desperate Latino outsider who immigrated years ago with stolen documents and hollow eyes, trying to navigate the violent neighborhoods of another nation that they call home. While most won't look at you, the subtle racial divisions in the spectrum of the places can leave you to wonder exactly who sees you as property and who sees you as a target.",
